===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ped-}}
The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|fecchen|t=to get and bring back, fetch; to come for, get and take away; to steal; to carry away to kill; to search for; to obtain, procure}} {{nb...|facch, facche, facchen, fach, fache, fæchen, fatche, feccean, fecche, fecchen, fecchenn, fecchyn, fech, fechchen, feche, fechen, fechin, fechyn, fetche, fetchen, fetchyn, focche, focchen, fochche, foche, fotche, vacche, vacchen, vecche, vecchen, vechchen,|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=fecchen|pos=v|id=MED15419}}</ref> from {{inh|en|ang|feċċan}}, {{m|ang|fæċċan}}, {{m|ang|feccean|t=to fetch, bring; to draw; to gain, take; to seek}}, a variant of {{m|ang|fetian}}, {{m|ang|fatian|t=to bring near, fetch; to acquire, obtain; to bring on, induce; to fetch a wife, marry}}<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=69591|date=1895|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Lexico|alt=fetch{{sup|1}}|pos=v}}</ref> and possibly related to {{cog|ang|facian}}, {{m|ang|fācian|t=to acquire, obtain; to try to obtain; to get; to get to, reach}}, both from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*fatōną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*fatjaną|t=to hold, seize; to fetch}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ped-|t=to step, walk; to fall, stumble}}. The English word is cognate with {{cog|nl|vatten|t=to apprehend, catch; to grasp; to understand}}, {{cog|en|fet|t=(''obsolete'') to fetch}}, {{cog|fo|fata|t=to grasp, understand}}, {{cog|sv|fatta|t=to grasp, understand}}, {{cog|de|fassen|t=to catch, grasp; to capture, seize}}, {{cog|is|feta|t=to go, step}}, {{cog|fy|fetsje|t=to grasp}}.

The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from the verb.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.{{sup|1}}''|noformat=yes|id=69588|date=1895|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Lexico|alt=fetch{{sup|1}}|pos=n}}</ref>

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[retrieve]]; to bear towards; to go and [[get]].
#* {{RQ:KJV|1 Kings|xvii|11-12|text=He called to her, and said, '''Fetch''' me, I pray thee, a little water in a vessel, that I may drink.}}
#* {{RQ:Grahame Wind in the Willows|passage=When they got home, the Rat made a bright fire in the parlour, and planted the Mole in an arm-chair in front of it, having '''fetched''' down a dressing-gown and slippers for him, and told him river stories till supper-time.}}
# To obtain as price or equivalent; to sell for.
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|chapter=3|passage=Our native horses{{...}} were held in small esteem, and '''fetched''' low prices.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913|author={{w|Joseph C. Lincoln}}|chapter=3
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL5535161W Mr. Pratt's Patients]
|passage=My hopes wa'n't disappointed. I never saw clams thicker than they was along them inshore flats. I filled my dreener in no time, and then it come to me that 'twouldn't be a bad idee to get a lot more, take 'em with me to Wellmouth, and peddle 'em out. Clams was fairly scarce over that side of the bay and ought to '''fetch''' a fair price.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-08-03|volume=408|issue=8847|magazine={{w|The Economist}}
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21582516-worlds-thirst-oil-could-be-nearing-peak-bad-news-producers-excellent Yesterday’s fuel]
|passage=The dawn of the oil age was fairly recent. Although the stuff was used to waterproof boats in the Middle East 6,000 years ago, extracting it in earnest began only in 1859 after an oil strike in Pennsylvania. The first barrels of crude '''fetched''' $18 (around $450 at today’s prices).}}
#: {{ux|en|If you put some new tyres on it, and clean it up a bit, the car should '''fetch''' about $5,000}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} To bring or get within reach by going; to reach; to arrive at; to attain; to reach by sailing.
#: {{ux|en|to '''fetch''' headway or sternway}}
#* {{RQ:Homer Chapman Odysseys|passage=Meantime flew our ships, and straight we '''fetched''' / The siren's isle.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To bring oneself; to make headway; to veer; as, to fetch about; to fetch to windward.
# {{lb|en|rare|literary}} To take (a breath), to heave (a sigh)
#* {{RQ:Conrad Heart of Darkness|part=I|passage=The hurt nigger moaned feebly somewhere near by, and then '''fetched''' a deep sigh that made me mend my pace away from there.}}
# To cause to come; to bring to a particular state.
#* '''1879''', {{w|William Barnes}}, ''A Witch''
#*: They couldn't '''fetch''' the butter in the churn.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To recall from a swoon; to revive; sometimes with ''to''.
#: {{ux|en|to '''fetch''' a man to}}
#* {{RQ:Bacon Sylva Sylvarum|passage='''Fetching''' men again when they swoon.}}
# To reduce; to throw.
#* '''1692''', {{w|Robert South}}, sermon 28
#*: The sudden trip in wrestling that '''fetches''' a man to the ground.
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To accomplish; to achieve; to perform, with certain objects or actions.
#: {{ux|en|to '''fetch''' a compass;&emsp; to '''fetch''' a leap}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Cymbeline|act=I|scene=i|passage=I'll '''fetch''' a turn about the garden.}}
#* {{RQ:Jonson Chloridia}}
#*: Ixion{{...}}turn'd dancer, does nothing but cut capreols, '''fetch''' friskals, and leads lavaltoes
#* '''1692''', {{w|Robert South}}, sermon 28
#*: He '''fetches''' his blow quick and sure.
# {{lb|en|nautical|transitive}} To make (a pump) draw water by pouring water into the top and working the handle.

