====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|also|figuratively}} An [[act#Noun|act]] of [[#Verb|fetching]], of [[bring]]ing something from a [[distance#Noun|distance]].
## {{lb|en|computing|specifically}} An act of fetching [[data]].
##: {{ux|en|a '''fetch''' from a cache}}
# The [[object#Noun|object]] of [[fetching#Noun|fetching]]; the [[source#Noun|source]] of an [[attraction]]; a [[force#Noun|force]], [[propensity]], or [[quality#Noun|quality]] which [[attract]]s.
# An area over which wind is blowing (over [[water]]) and generating waves.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1977|author=Coastal Engineering Research Center (U.S.)|title=Shore Protection Manual|page=29
|text=When a '''fetch''' is close to land, this variability will alter anticipated wind directions and velocities.}}
# The [[length]] of such an area; the distance a wave can travel across a body of water (without obstruction).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|title=Résumés
|text=From recently completed radar maps of the Brazilian Amazon I determined the shape, maximum '''fetch''' and width and orientation of all the lakes greater than 100 meters across in the floodplain {{...}} }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Andrew Rose|author2=Sandra Rose|title='Man Overboard!': The HMAS Nizam Tragedy|publisher=Red Rose Books|isbn=9780977523801|page=48
|text=For example, a steady wind of 40-50 kilometres/hour - a Force 6 strong breeze - blowing for 12 hours over an initially calm sea and traversing a '''fetch''' of 1000 kilometres could produce a significant wave height {{nb...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Yoshimi Goda|title=Random Seas and Design of Maritime Structures|publisher=World Scientific|isbn=9789814282390|page=66
|text=Wind waves continue to grow within the fetch area, [...] A graphical wave hindcasting method by means of Wilson's fetch diagrams produced an estimate of ''H''<sub>1/3</sub> = 9.4 m and ''T''<sub>1/3</sub> = 12.3 s over the '''fetch''' of about 1,800 km on the 7th of April.}}
# A [[stratagem]] or [[trick#Noun|trick]]; an [[artifice]].
#: {{synonyms|en|contrivance|dodge}}
#* '''1665''', Robert South, "Jesus of Nazareth proved the true and only promised Messiah", in ''Twelve Sermons Preached Upon Several Occasions'', Volume 3, 6th Edition, 1727:
#*: Every little '''fetch''' of wit and criticism.
#* {{RQ:Richardson Clarissa|letter=29|text=And as to your cant of living single, nobody will believe you. This is one of your '''''fetches''''' to avoid complying with your duty […].}}

