===Etymology 2===
{{unknown|en|Origin uncertain}}; the following possibilities have been suggested:

* From {{m|en|fetch-life|t=(''obsolete, rare'') a deity, spirit, etc., who guides the soul of a dead person to the afterlife; a psychopomp}}.<ref name="OED">{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.{{sup|2}}''|noformat=yes|id=69589|date=1895|nodot=yes}}; {{R:Lexico|alt=fetch{{sup|2}}|pos=n}}</ref><ref>{{R:OED Online|entry=† fetch-life|pos=n|id=69598|date=1895}}</ref>
* From the supposed {{inh|en|ang|*fæcce|t=evil spirit formerly thought to sit on the chest of a sleeping person; a mare}}.<ref name="OED"/>
* From {{der|en|sga|fáith|t=seer, soothsayer}}.<ref>{{cite-journal|author=William Sayers|title=A Hiberno-Norse Etymology for English ''Fetch'': ‘Apparition of a Living Person’|journal=[[w:ANQ (journal)|ANQ: A Quarterly Journal of Short Articles, Notes and Reviews]]|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher={{w|Heldref Publications}}; Lexington, Ky.: {{w|University Press of Kentucky}}|year=2017|volume=30|issue=4|pages=205–209|doi=10.1080/0895769X.2017.1336073|issn=0895-769X|oclc=613305589}}.</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|originally|Ireland|dialectal}} The [[apparition]] of a [[living#Adjective|living]] [[person]]; a person's [[double#Noun|double]], the [[sight]] of which is supposedly a [[sign#Noun|sign]] that they are [[fate#Verb|fated]] to [[die#Verb|die]] soon, a [[doppelganger]]; a {{l|en|wraith|t=a person's [[likeness]] seen just after their [[death]]; a [[ghost#Noun|ghost]], a [[spectre]]}}. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Martin Chuzzlewit|chapter=The Reader is Brought into Communication with Some Professional Persons, and Sheds a Tear over the Filial Piety of Good Mr. Jonas|page=236|passage=In these dilapidated articles of dress she had, on principle, arrayed herself, time out of mind, on such occasions as the present; for this at once expressed a decent amount of veneration for the deceased, and invited the next of kin to present her with a fresher set of weeds: an appeal so frequently successful, that the very '''fetch''' and ghost of Mrs. Gamp, bonnet and all, might be seen hanging up, at any hour of the day, in at least a dozen of the second-hand clothes shops around Holborn.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Gordon Bottomley|authorlink=Gordon Bottomley|title=Midsummer Eve|location=Harting, Petersfield, Hampshire|publisher=Pear Tree Press|year=1905|oclc=12810083|newversion=republished in|title2=King Lear’s Wife, The Crier by Night, The Riding to Lithend, Midsummer Eve, Laodice and Danaë: Plays|location2=London|publisher2=[[w:Constable & Robinson|Constable & Company]]|year2=1920|page2=159|pageurl2=https://archive.org/details/kinglearswifecri01bott/page/159/mode/1up|oclc2=989162481|passage=I think it was a '''fetch'''. [...] Folk say a '''fetch''' is seen at its departing / From a cold house whence it shall lead a soul; / But this comes like a child-birth closing in, / And so perchance it does but signify / The consciousness of death that breaks in all.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Sterling Andrus Leonard|chapter=Bibliography of Plays for Reading in High Schools|editor=Sterling Andrus Leonard|title=The Atlantic Book of Modern Plays:{{nb...|Edited with Introduction, Comment and Annotated Bibliography}}|location=Boston, Mass.|publisher=[[w:Grove Atlantic|The Atlantic Monthly Press]]|year=1921|page=300|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/atlanticbookofmo008932mbp/page/n321/mode/1up|oclc=1154741|passage=Several farm maidservants meet to see their future lovers' spirits on Midsummer Eve, but see only the "'''fetch'''" or double of one of them, foretelling her death.|footer={{small|A summary of Gordon Bottomley’s play ''Midsummer Eve''.}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971|author=Richard Carpenter|authorlink=Richard Carpenter (screenwriter)|title=Catweazle and the Magic Zodiac|publisher=Puffin Books|location=Harmondsworth|page=80|text="If you're after some money," said the millionaire, "move that cat out of the way." Catweazle shook his head. "Mayhap 'tis a '''fetch'''." Victor was very taken aback. "A '''fetch'''?" he gulped. "That's a witch in the shape of a cat, isn't it?" "Ay," said Catweazle calmly.}}

